.lua
book.chapter(30, "Introspection and language tooling")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Expose stages without conflating them"
.pp
Frontend wraps tokenization, parsing, type inference, type formatting, and
isolated evaluation. These operations support editors, teaching tools, and
small language services written in MetaML itself. A token query is different
from a parse query, and type checking is different from evaluation. Keeping
the stage explicit allows a client to ask a useful question without causing
unnecessary execution or discarding a more precise earlier diagnostic.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "inference query" (Frontend.type_of "fn x -> x + 1")
  (Ok "Int -> Int")
Test.equal "nested evaluation" (Frontend.eval "double x := x * 2\ndouble 21")
  (Ok "42")
Test.check "parse failure is data" (Result.is_err (Frontend.parse_expr "("))]=],
    "Language stages as callable services")
.endlua
.pp
Frontend.eval uses a fresh nested session with core primitives. It does not
clone the caller's definitions, standard-library bindings, or installed host
modules. Output is forwarded to the calling session. A definition made in
the nested program does not become a parent binding. This is isolation of
language state, not proof of purity or process-level security: core effects
can still occur when the nested program executes.
.sh 2 "Textual type operations are useful tools"
.pp
Type.normalize gives consistent variable naming, equal compares normalized
structure, unify computes a compatible type or error, and instance_of tests
a directional relationship. Arity counts function arguments in the parsed
type. These operations are useful for teaching unification, comparing
signatures, and building lightweight interface reports. Unknown names are
not automatically accepted as existing session type constructors in every
tooling context; inspect the operation's actual environment contract.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "unify function types"
  (Type.unify "'a -> Int" "String -> 'b") (Ok "String -> Int")
Test.check "renamed variables" (Type.equal "'a -> 'a" "'b -> 'b")
Test.equal "curried arity" (Type.arity "Int -> String -> Bool") (Ok 2)
Test.check "incompatible types" (Result.is_err (Type.unify "Int" "String"))]=],
    "Type relationships can be inspected without evaluating a program")
.endlua
.pp
Equality and instantiation are different questions. Two type schemes can
have the same normalized structure, while a concrete type can be an instance
of a more general scheme without being equal to it. An editor suggesting
candidate functions must choose the relationship appropriate to the task.
Simply comparing printed type strings can confuse irrelevant variable names
with genuine structural differences.
.sh 2 "Module introspection respects visibility"
.pp
Module provides queries for known modules, members, submodules, types,
signatures, and member types. It reports the checked visibility surface;
it is not a mechanism for recovering a sealed representation. Some host
primitive namespaces are registered paths rather than ordinary source
structures, so the primitive registry and source-module registry need not
have identical membership. A discovery tool should label which registry
each item came from.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.check "portable source module" (Module.exists "List")
Test.check "member listing" (Option.is_some (Module.members "List"))
Test.check "member type" (Option.is_some (Module.member_type "List" "map"))
Test.check "missing module" (not (Module.exists "Definitely_missing"))]=],
    "Queries over the caller's checked module environment")
.endlua
.pp
Introspection can generate documentation, completion candidates, and migration
reports. It cannot replace behavioral documentation. A function type does
not tell you whether sorting is stable, whether traversal stops at the first
error, or whether a returned handle shares storage. Combine discovered
signatures with authored contracts and executable examples. This book follows
that principle rather than presenting a raw registry dump as a complete guide.
.sh 2 "Loading is an effectful tooling operation"
.pp
Loader inspects search paths, resolves specifications, lists loaded files,
and explicitly loads source into the caller's session. Resolve is a query;
load mutates the environment and can execute effects. Grant Loader capabilities
only when source loading is part of the intended tool. A resolved path also
does not guarantee that the file's contents remain unchanged before a later
load; reproducible tooling should control its source snapshot.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.check "standard source resolution" (Option.is_some (Loader.resolve "list"))
Test.check "unknown source" (Option.is_none (Loader.resolve "missing-book-module-xyz"))]=],
    "Resolution can be queried separately from execution")
.endlua
.pp
For an editor, keep an explicit document version with every analysis request.
A delayed result for an earlier buffer must not overwrite diagnostics for
a newer edit. Session isolation can help separate analyses, but the host
still owns scheduling and publication. The same principle applies to generated
documentation: record which interpreter and source revision produced the
report so a reader can reproduce its view of the API.
.sh 2 "Build a staged workbench"
.lua
book.chain({"Document snapshot", "Token and parse queries", "Type and module queries", "Explicit execution"},
    "A workbench separates inspection from execution")
.endlua
.pp
A useful workbench offers syntax inspection before evaluation, shows inferred
types beside expressions, and performs execution only through an explicit
action. Parse failures should retain source context rather than becoming
empty trees. Type failures should not trigger fallback evaluation. Execution
results should distinguish returned values, output bytes, and diagnostics,
because those are separate channels that a user interface may present
differently.
.pp
Avoid repeated full-session mutation merely to answer a local query. A fresh
analysis context can make results independent of stale interactive bindings.
For a real project, however, imports and public interfaces supply necessary
context, so an isolated expression query is not automatically equivalent to
checking the expression in its original module. State the analysis environment
alongside the result when that distinction affects interpretation.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the Frontend, Type, Module, and Loader host modules in both dialects.
Build a report listing one module's public members and types, then add an
explicitly separate action for evaluating a sample expression. Test that
a definition evaluated through Frontend does not appear in the caller.
Describe how the report should distinguish source modules from registered
primitive namespaces, and which authored behavioral information still cannot
be inferred from either registry.
